LESSONS = [
{"code": "EGCP 446", "sem": "Spring 2021", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Advanced Digital Design with Verilog", "sub": "EGCP 446 · Dr. Rakesh Mahto · Artix-7 FPGA",
  "say": "Spring twenty twenty one. Advanced Digital Design with Verilog. Same hardware thinking as V H D L, but in the language most of industry uses, and on a real F P G A board with all the messy real world problems that come with it."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Blocking vs non-blocking: the rule", "code": "// sequential: NON-BLOCKING  <=\nalways @(posedge clk) begin\n  b <= a;        // both right-hand sides sampled first,\n  c <= b;        // then updated together → 2-stage shift register\nend\n\n// with BLOCKING = :  b = a; c = b;   → c gets a immediately\n//                                       (one flip-flop collapses!)\n\n// combinational: BLOCKING = inside always @(*), assign every output",
  "say": "If you remember one rule from Verilog, make it this one. In clocked blocks, use non blocking assignment, the less than equals arrow. All the right hand sides are sampled first, then everything updates together, just like real flip-flops that all capture on the same clock edge. So b gets a, and c gets the old b. That's a two stage shift register. Now write the same thing with blocking assignment, the plain equals sign. b becomes a immediately, then c gets the new b, which is a. One of your flip-flops just disappeared. For combinational logic inside always at star, use blocking assignment, and give every output a value on every path, or you'll get the same accidental latch we talked about in V H D L."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Clock divider: 100 MHz → 1 Hz", "code": "100,000,000 cycles per second → toggle every 50,000,000\n\nreg [25:0] cnt;   // 2^26 = 67M > 50M\nalways @(posedge clk)\n  if (cnt == 50_000_000 - 1) begin cnt <= 0; slow <= ~slow; end\n  else cnt <= cnt + 1;\n\nBetter practice: make a 1-cycle ENABLE pulse,\nkeep everything on the main clock.",
  "say": "A one hundred megahertz board clock is far too fast to see an L E D blink. To get one hertz, the output must toggle twice per second, so once every fifty million cycles. How many bits does the counter need? Two to the twenty five is about thirty three million, too small. Two to the twenty six is about sixty seven million, enough. So it's a twenty six bit counter that counts to fifty million minus one, then resets and flips the slow output. That's your Lab four. A professional tip: rather than creating a new clock from logic, which confuses timing analysis, generate a one cycle enable pulse and keep every flip-flop on the main clock."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Real-world inputs", "pts": ["Buttons bounce for ~1–10 ms → many false edges", "Debounce: require input stable N ms (counter), then edge-detect", "Async inputs: 2-flip-flop synchronizer avoids metastability", "Display multiplexing: cycle digits at ~1 kHz (Project B)"],
  "say": "Simulation hides problems that real hardware doesn't. Press a mechanical button and the contacts bounce, opening and closing for a few milliseconds. To a one hundred megahertz circuit, one press looks like dozens. A debouncer only accepts the new value once the input has stayed stable for several milliseconds, then detects the edge. Also, any input not tied to your clock can change right as a flip-flop samples it, putting the flip-flop into a metastable state, halfway between zero and one. Passing it through two flip-flops in a row gives it time to settle. And to drive four seven segment digits with one set of segment wires, light one digit at a time, around a thousand times a second. Persistence of vision makes them all look on. That was your Project B."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Memories and the implementation flow", "pts": ["reg [7:0] mem [0:255];  synchronous write → block RAM", "ROM: case statement or $readmemh", "Synthesis → place & route → bitstream", ".xdc constraints · timing report: negative slack = too slow"],
  "say": "Memories in Verilog are arrays of registers. Write them in the standard pattern, a synchronous write on the clock edge, and the tools map them onto the F P G A's dedicated block RAM instead of thousands of flip-flops. ROMs can be a case statement or a file loaded with read mem H. That's your Lab five. Then the flow: synthesis turns your R T L into lookup tables and flip-flops, place and route positions them on the chip and wires them, and you get a bitstream to program the board. The constraints file maps your ports to physical pins and tells the tools your clock speed. Always read the timing report. Negative slack means some path is too slow for your clock, and the design may fail on real hardware even though it simulated perfectly."},
]},

{"code": "EGCP 450", "sem": "Spring 2021", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Embedded Processor Interfacing", "sub": "EGCP 450 · Dr. Mike Turi · MSP432 (ARM Cortex-M4F)",
  "say": "Embedded Processor Interfacing, E G C P four fifty. No operating system, no safety net. Just you, an ARM Cortex M four, and its registers. This is the most computer engineering course of the whole degree."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "ARM assembly: toggle an LED", "code": "        LDR   R0, =P1OUT      ; address of port 1 output register\nloop    LDRB  R1, [R0]        ; read current value\n        EOR   R1, R1, #0x01   ; flip bit 0 (red LED)\n        STRB  R1, [R0]        ; write it back\n        BL    delay           ; call subroutine (return addr → LR)\n        B     loop\n\ndelay   PUSH  {R4, LR}\n        ...                   ; burn cycles\n        POP   {R4, PC}        ; restore + return",
  "say": "The Cortex M is a load store machine: you can only do math on registers, so you load from memory, compute, and store back. Peripheral registers live at memory addresses, so turning on an L E D is just a memory write. Here we load the address of port one's output register, read it, flip bit zero with exclusive or, and write it back. That's a read modify write, so we don't disturb the other pins. B L calls the delay subroutine and puts the return address in the link register. Inside, push saves the registers we'll use plus L R onto the stack, and pop restores them, loading the saved return address straight into the program counter. That's how function calls, nesting and recursion work at the lowest level. By convention, arguments go in R zero to R three and the result comes back in R zero."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "SysTick timing", "code": "Bus clock 48 MHz → 1 tick = 20.83 ns\n1 ms = 48,000 ticks →  RELOAD = 48000 − 1 = 47999\n\nSysTick->LOAD = 47999;\nSysTick->VAL  = 0;\nSysTick->CTRL = 0x05;          // enable, use core clock\nwhile ((SysTick->CTRL & 0x10000) == 0) { }  // wait for COUNTFLAG",
  "say": "Delay loops waste the C P U and are imprecise. SysTick is a twenty four bit down counter built into every Cortex M. It counts down once per bus clock, and when it hits zero it sets a flag or fires an interrupt, then reloads. To get one millisecond at forty eight megahertz, you need forty eight thousand ticks, so the reload value is forty seven thousand nine hundred ninety nine. It's minus one because the count includes zero. Call that one millisecond wait in a loop and you have precise timing. That's how your traffic light controller held each light for exactly the right time."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Table-driven FSM: the traffic light", "code": "struct State { uint8_t out; uint16_t wait_ms; uint8_t next[4]; };\nconst struct State FSM[] = {\n /* goN   */ {0x21, 3000, {goN, waitN, goN, waitN}},\n /* waitN */ {0x22,  500, {goE, goE,  goE, goE  }},\n /* goE   */ {0x0C, 3000, {goE, goE,  waitE, waitE}},\n ...\n};\nwhile (1) { LIGHTS = FSM[s].out; wait(FSM[s].wait_ms);\n            s = FSM[s].next[read_sensors()]; }",
  "say": "Your Lab three traffic light used a table driven Moore machine, which is the cleanest way to write a controller in C. Each state is a row in a table: what to output, how long to wait, and which state to go to next for each possible input combination. The main loop never changes. Output the lights, wait, read the sensors, look up the next state. To change the behavior, like adding the pedestrian walk and blinking don't walk states you built, you edit the table, not the logic. Elevators, vending machines and washing machines are all written this way."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "DAC sound and serial I/O", "pts": ["Binary-weighted DAC: V_out ∝ Σ bᵢ 2ⁱ", "Sine table of N samples, one per interrupt", "Tone f = interrupt rate / N  → 440 Hz × 32 = 14.08 kHz", "UART (async, baud) · SPI (fast, 4-wire) · I²C (2-wire, addressed)"],
  "say": "Your digital piano turned bits into sound. A four bit digital to analog converter is just four resistors weighted one, two, four and eight, summing the output pins into one voltage. Store one period of a sine wave as a table of, say, thirty two samples, and output the next sample on every timer interrupt. The tone's frequency is the interrupt rate divided by the table length. For an A at four hundred forty hertz with thirty two samples, you interrupt at fourteen point oh eight kilohertz. Sampling theory from three seventy one in action. Finally, serial communication. A U A R T sends start bit, eight data bits and stop bit, with both sides agreeing on a baud rate. S P I uses a shared clock and is fast, good for displays. I squared C uses just two wires and addresses many sensors on one bus."},
]},

{"code": "EGCP 470", "sem": "Spring 2021", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Senior Design I", "sub": "EGCP 470 · Team G · Robotic Arm Assistant",
  "say": "Senior Design one. Your team, Team G, with Juan, Alec and Ahmed, proposed a robotic arm assistant: a voice commanded arm that finds and fetches tools, built on a Raspberry Pi with a camera. Your role was the arm itself. Let's talk about how real engineering projects get from an idea to a plan."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Writing requirements you can test", "code": "Vague:   \"The arm should recognize tools.\"\n\nTestable:\n  R1  Detect a stapler at 0.3–0.6 m with ≥ 90% success\n      under normal office lighting.\n  R2  Deliver the tool within 15 s of the voice command.\n  R3  Total cost ≤ $600   (your budget: ~$569)\n  R4  Arm stops if commanded outside its safe envelope.",
  "say": "The most important skill in a capstone is writing requirements you can actually test. The arm should recognize tools isn't a requirement. You can't fail it, so you can't pass it either. Rewrite it with numbers: detect a stapler between point three and point six meters with at least ninety percent success under office lighting. Deliver the tool within fifteen seconds of the command. Stay under the budget, which for your team was about five hundred sixty nine dollars. And include safety. Each requirement now tells you exactly what test to run at the end. Separate the must haves from the nice to haves, because something will always have to be cut."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Planning and risk", "pts": ["Block diagram: define interfaces early → parallel work", "Gantt chart, milestones, critical path", "Risk register: risk · likelihood · impact · mitigation", "e.g. voice recognition unreliable → fallback: button menu"],
  "say": "Next, the system architecture. Draw a block diagram of the subsystems and define the interfaces between them, the power, the data and the mechanical connections, as early as possible. Your team split the work into voice detection, object detection, path assignment and the arm, which only works if everyone agrees on what passes between those blocks. Then a schedule with milestones, and a risk register. For each risk, estimate likelihood and impact, and plan a mitigation. For example: risk, voice recognition is unreliable in a noisy lab. Mitigation: a simple button menu as a fallback. Biweekly reports like the one in your folder, covering what's done, what's next, what's blocking us and where the budget stands, are exactly how industry tracks projects."},
]},

{"code": "Data side projects", "sem": "Spring 2021", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Data Wrangling in R & Power BI", "sub": "Tweet analysis · Power BI · Data structures",
  "say": "A quick side lesson on the data projects in your Other folder. You analyzed about fifteen thousand tweets in R, and combined stock price data in Power BI. The skills are the same ones as S Q L, with different syntax."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Tidy text in five lines", "code": "tweets %>%\n  unnest_tokens(word, text) %>%      # one row per word\n  anti_join(stop_words) %>%          # drop 'the', 'and', ...\n  filter(!word %in% c(\"ev\",\"electric\",\"gas\")) %>%\n  count(word, sort = TRUE)           # word frequencies\n\n≈ SQL:  SELECT word, COUNT(*) ... WHERE ... GROUP BY word ORDER BY 2 DESC",
  "say": "Here's your R pipeline. The pipe operator passes each result to the next step. Split each tweet into one row per word. Remove stop words like the and and with an anti join. Filter out your search terms, since of course they're the most common words. Then count and sort. Look at the comment underneath: it's the same as a S Q L query with where, group by and order by. Filter, select, arrange, mutate and summarize are the five verbs of data wrangling in any language. Your result was interesting: lots of tweets aimed at Elon Musk, and Toyota's Prius marketing. The natural next steps are T F I D F, which finds words distinctive to a group, and sentiment analysis."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Hash tables in one slide", "pts": ["h(key) = key mod m → slot", "54, 26, 93, 17, 77, 31 with m = 11 → slots 10, 4, 5, 6, 0, 9", "Collision: 44 mod 11 = 0 = 77 mod 11", "Resolve by chaining or probing · keep load factor low → O(1)"],
  "say": "And one data structure from your slides, because it's everywhere: the hash table. Use a hash function to turn a key into a slot number, for example the key mod eleven. Fifty four mod eleven is ten, twenty six is four, ninety three is five, seventeen is six, seventy seven is zero, thirty one is nine. Lookup is now one computation, constant time, no searching. But forty four mod eleven is also zero, the same slot as seventy seven. That's a collision. Either keep a little list in each slot, called chaining, or probe to the next free slot. Keep the table less than about three quarters full and lookups stay constant time on average. Python dictionaries, database hash indexes and caches all work this way."},
]},

{"code": "EGCP 447", "sem": "Fall 2021", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Hardware Security", "sub": "EGCP 447 · Dr. Jaya Dofe · Cyber-Physical Systems",
  "say": "Fall twenty twenty one. Hardware Security, E G C P four forty seven. Software security assumes the chip is honest. This course asks: what if it isn't?"},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Anatomy of a hardware Trojan", "code": "-- original:   out <= (a nand b);\n-- infected:\nprocess(clk) begin\n  if rising_edge(clk) then\n    if (a='1' and b='1' and c='0') then cnt <= cnt + 1; end if;\n  end if;\nend process;\ntrigger <= '1' when cnt = 255 else '0';   -- rare!\nout <= (a nand b) xor trigger;            -- payload: flip output",
  "say": "A hardware Trojan has two parts: a trigger and a payload. The trigger has to be rare, so it never fires during normal testing. The payload does the damage. In this example, inspired by your C seventeen assignment, a counter quietly counts how many times a rare input combination appears. Only after the two hundred fifty fifth occurrence does the trigger go high, and the payload flips the gate's output. Functional tests that run a few thousand random vectors will almost never see it. Payloads can leak a secret key, disable the chip, or slowly degrade it. Who could insert one? A third party I P vendor, a compromised design tool, or an untrusted foundry overseas. Detection methods include tests targeting rarely activated nodes, and side channel fingerprinting, comparing power and timing to a known good golden chip."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Side-channel attacks", "pts": ["Timing: early-exit compare leaks how many bytes matched", "Power: SPA reads square vs multiply in RSA directly", "DPA/CPA: correlate thousands of traces with Hamming weight", "Defenses: constant-time code, masking, hiding, noise"],
  "say": "Side channel attacks don't break the math. They listen to the hardware. Timing first: a password check that stops at the first wrong character takes longer the more characters you got right, so an attacker can guess one character at a time. Power: in R S A, square and multiply operations draw visibly different power, so a single power trace can spell out the key bits. That's simple power analysis. Differential and correlation power analysis go further: record thousands of traces, guess one key byte, predict the Hamming weight, the number of one bits, of an intermediate value, and see which guess correlates with the measured power. The right guess lights up. That's how real smart cards and car key fobs have been broken. Defenses include constant time code, masking intermediate values with randomness, and adding noise."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Hamming (7,4): fix a flipped bit", "code": "positions:  1  2  3  4  5  6  7\n            p1 p2 d1 p4 d2 d3 d4\np1 covers 1,3,5,7 · p2 covers 2,3,6,7 · p4 covers 4,5,6,7\n\ndata 1011 → codeword 0 1 1 0 0 1 1\nreceived     0 1 1 0 1 1 1   (bit 5 flipped)\nsyndrome: p4 fails, p2 ok, p1 fails → 1 0 1 = 5 → flip bit 5 ✓",
  "say": "Your project built a Hamming seven four encoder and decoder. Four data bits, three parity bits at positions one, two and four. Each parity bit checks the positions whose binary index contains that bit: p one covers positions one, three, five and seven; p two covers two, three, six and seven; p four covers four, five, six and seven. Encode the data one, zero, one, one, and the codeword is zero, one, one, zero, zero, one, one. Now suppose bit five gets flipped in transmission. The receiver rechecks every parity group. The p four group fails, the p two group passes, and the p one group fails. Read those as binary: one, zero, one, which is five. The syndrome literally points at the broken bit. Flip it back and the data is recovered. This is why server memory uses E C C to survive cosmic ray bit flips."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Protecting designs and IoT", "pts": ["Logic locking: key gates — wrong key → wrong function", "SAT attack prunes keys using a working chip as oracle", "Camouflaging · split manufacturing · PUFs for chip fingerprints", "IoT: default passwords (Mirai), no updates → secure boot, root of trust"],
  "say": "How do designers protect their chips from an untrusted foundry? Logic locking inserts extra X O R gates controlled by a secret key, so the circuit only works correctly once the right key is loaded after fabrication. Researchers then found the SAT attack, which uses a working chip as an oracle to rule out wrong keys very efficiently, kicking off an arms race of stronger locking schemes. Camouflaging makes different gates look identical under a microscope. Split manufacturing sends only part of the layers to the untrusted foundry. And physical unclonable functions use tiny manufacturing variations as an unforgeable fingerprint. Finally, I o T. Billions of cheap devices with default passwords and no update path. The Mirai botnet hijacked hundreds of thousands of cameras and routers that way. The fixes start in hardware: secure boot, a hardware root of trust, and signed firmware updates."},
]},

{"code": "EGCP 471", "sem": "Fall 2021", "slides": [
 {"t": "title", "h": "Senior Design II: Vision-Guided Arm", "sub": "EGCP 471 · Python · OpenCV · Raspberry Pi",
  "say": "Senior Design two. Your team finished the robotic arm: a Raspberry Pi camera, custom trained Haar cascade detectors for a stapler and a screwdriver, and Python code that sweeps the arm, spots the tool and moves toward it. Let's understand how that detector really works."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Haar features and the integral image", "code": "Haar feature = sum(white rectangle) − sum(dark rectangle)\n\nIntegral image: II(x,y) = sum of all pixels above-left of (x,y)\nAny rectangle sum = II(D) − II(B) − II(C) + II(A)   ← 4 lookups!\n\n24×24 window → 162,336 possible features (your training log)",
  "say": "A Haar feature is a simple pattern of adjacent rectangles: the sum of the pixels in the white part minus the sum in the dark part. It responds to edges, lines and contrast, like the dark slot along a stapler's side. To make these cheap, the detector precomputes an integral image, where every pixel holds the sum of everything above and to the left. Then the sum of any rectangle, of any size, takes just four lookups: D minus B minus C plus A. In a twenty four by twenty four window there are over one hundred sixty thousand possible features. Your training log reported exactly one hundred sixty two thousand three hundred thirty six. Far too many to use, so the next step is to choose."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Boosting + cascade: why it's fast", "code": "AdaBoost: pick the few features that best separate\n          stapler vs background, weight them → stage classifier\n\nYour stapler cascade: 11 stages,\n  per stage: hit rate 0.995, false-alarm 0.44\n\noverall detection ≈ 0.995^11 ≈ 0.946\noverall false-alarm ≈ 0.44^11 ≈ 0.00012 per window",
  "say": "AdaBoost picks a handful of the most useful features and combines them into a stage classifier, reweighting the training examples after each pick so the next feature focuses on the mistakes. Then comes the cascade, the real speed trick. Stages are chained, and the early stages are tiny. Most windows in an image are obviously background and get rejected in the first stage or two, so only promising windows reach the bigger later stages. Your final stapler cascade had eleven stages, each tuned to keep ninety nine and a half percent of staplers while letting through at most forty four percent of background. Multiply across the stages. Detection is about point nine nine five to the eleventh, roughly ninety five percent. False alarms are point four four to the eleventh, about one in eight thousand windows. That's the Bayes lesson from three twenty three again: per window rates multiply out across hundreds of thousands of windows per frame."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Making it work in the real world", "pts": ["detectMultiScale(scaleFactor, minNeighbors, minSize)", "Negatives from the robot's actual environment", "Hard-negative mining: add false positives back as negatives", "Today: CNN detectors (YOLO, MobileNet) on edge hardware"],
  "say": "At run time, detect multi scale slides the window across the image at many sizes. Scale factor sets how finely it steps through sizes. Min neighbors demands that several overlapping detections agree, which raises it to cut false positives at the cost of some misses. Your code put these on trackbars so you could tune them live, and that was a smart move. The biggest lever, though, is the data. Your create data script saved every tenth frame and threw out blurry ones, which is good practice. Negatives should come from the exact place the robot works. And when the detector fires on the wrong thing, add that image back as a negative and retrain. That's called hard negative mining. Today, small neural network detectors like YOLO run in real time on a Raspberry Pi five, and they'd be the natural upgrade."},
 {"t": "ex", "h": "Inverse kinematics: 2-link arm", "code": "L1 = L2 = 10 cm,   target (x, y) = (10, 10)\n\ncos θ2 = (x² + y² − L1² − L2²) / (2 L1 L2)\n       = (100 + 100 − 100 − 100) / 200 = 0   →  θ2 = 90°\nθ1 = atan2(y, x) − atan2(L2 sin θ2, L1 + L2 cos θ2)\n   = 45° − atan2(10, 10) = 45° − 45° = 0°\n\ncheck: link 1 along x to (10,0), link 2 straight up → (10,10) ✓",
  "say": "Once you see the tool, how does the arm reach it? Forward kinematics goes from joint angles to the hand's position. Inverse kinematics goes the other way, and it's what you actually need. For a two link arm with both links ten centimeters long, and a target at ten, ten: by the law of cosines, cosine of theta two is x squared plus y squared minus L one squared minus L two squared, over two L one L two. That's two hundred minus two hundred over two hundred, which is zero, so the elbow is at ninety degrees. Then theta one is the angle to the target, forty five degrees, minus the angle the second link adds, also forty five, which is zero. Check it: the first link lies flat along x to ten, zero, and the second points straight up to ten, ten. Correct. Real arms with more joints use four by four transformation matrices, linear algebra from two fifty B, closing the loop on your whole degree."},
]},

{"code": "The Big Picture", "sem": "", "slides": [
 {"t": "b", "h": "The stack you climbed", "pts": ["Physics → devices → circuits → logic → CPU", "→ assembly & bare metal → OS → applications & data", "→ a vision-guided robot that uses all of it", "Math, security and ethics run through every layer"],
  "say": "Let's step back and look at what you actually learned. Your degree climbed every layer of a computer. Physics explained charges and fields. Electronics turned that into diodes and transistors. Circuits taught you to analyze them. C MOS turned transistors into gates. V H D L and Verilog turned gates into systems. Computer design turned those systems into a processor. Embedded systems put you at the processor's bare metal, operating systems built the layer above it, and databases and software engineering built the applications on top. Your capstone used all of it at once. Running alongside were the math of linear algebra, probability and transforms, plus security and ethics."},
 {"t": "b", "h": "Ideas that keep coming back", "pts": ["State machines: 281, 446, 450, 362, 381's control unit", "Linearity: superposition, phasors, Fourier, eigenvectors", "Trade-offs: speed · power · area · cost · accuracy", "Concurrency: hardware, threads, interrupts, pipelines"],
  "say": "And notice the ideas that kept coming back. State machines appeared in at least five courses. Linearity, breaking a hard problem into simple pieces and adding them up, gave us superposition, phasors, Fourier series and eigenvectors. Every design was a trade-off: speed versus power, gain versus bandwidth, detection versus false alarms, cost versus benefit. And concurrency, many things happening at once, was both the source of hardware's power and the source of its hardest bugs. If you remember those four ideas, you remember the heart of computer engineering. That's the end of the review. Thanks for sitting through class one more time. Class dismissed."},
]},
]
